nointed, the Greek translation of the Hebrew word rendered “Messiah”, the official title of our Lord, occurring five hundred and fourteen times in the New Testament.","verses":1831,"father":"Joseph","mother":"Mary","v":[7,11]},{"id":"peter_2745","name":"Simon","also":"Cephas, Simeon","summary":"Originally called Simon (=Simeon, i.e., “hearing”), a very common Jewish name in the New Testament. He was the son of Jona (Matt. 16:17). His mother is nowhere named in Scripture.","verses":175,"father":"Jonas","mother":"","v":[16]},{"id":"john_1677","name":"John","also":"Boanerges","summary":"THE APOSTLE, brother of James the “Greater” (Matt. 4:21; 10:2; Mark 1:19; 3:17; 10:35). He was one, probably the younger, of the sons of Zebedee (Matt. 4:21) and Salome (Matt. 27:56; comp. Mark 15:40), and was born at Bethsaida.","verses":35,"father":"Zebedee","mother":"Mother of Zebedees children","v":[17]},{"id":"james_717","name":"James","also":"","summary":"The son of Zebedee and Salome; an elder brother of John the apostle. He was one of the twelve. He was by trade a fisherman, in partnership with Peter (Matt. 20:20; 27:56).","verses":18,"father":"Zebedee","mother":"Mother of Zebedees children","v":[17]},{"id":"zebedee_2998","name":"Zebedee","also":"","summary":"A Galilean fisherman, the husband of Salome, and the father of James and John, two of our Lord’s disciples (Matt. 4:21; 27:56; Mark 15:40). He seems to have been a man of some position in Capernaum, for he had two boats (Luke 5:4) and “hired servants” (Mark 1:20) of his own.","verses":11,"father":"","mother":"","v":[17]},{"id":"philip_2344","name":"Philip","also":"","summary":"One of the twelve apostles; a native of Bethsaida, “the city of Andrew and Peter” (John 1:44). He readily responded to the call of Jesus when first addressed to him (43), and forthwith brought Nathanael also to Jesus (45, 46).","verses":15,"father":"","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"andrew_264","name":"Andrew","also":"","summary":"Manliness, a Greek name; one of the apostles of our Lord. He was of Bethsaida in Galilee (John 1:44), and was the brother of Simon Peter (Matt. 4:18; 10:2).","verses":12,"father":"Jonas","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"thomas_2851","name":"Thomas","also":"Didymus","summary":"Twin, one of the twelve (Matt. 10:3; Mark 3:18, etc.). He was also called Didymus (John 11:16; 20:24), which is the Greek equivalent of the Hebrew name.","verses":12,"father":"","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"matthew_1971","name":"Matthew","also":"Levi","summary":"Gift of God, a common Jewish name after the Exile. He was the son of Alphaeus, and was a publican or tax-gatherer at Capernaum. On one occasion Jesus, coming up from the side of the lake, passed the custom-house where Matthew was seated, and said to him, “Follow me.”","verses":8,"father":"Alphaeus","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"james_718","name":"James","also":"","summary":"The son of Alphaeus, or Cleopas, “the brother” or near kinsman or cousin of our Lord (Gal. 1:18, 19), called James “the Less,” or “the Little,” probably because he was of low stature.","verses":5,"father":"Alphaeus","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"lebbaeus_1815","name":"Lebbaeus","also":"Judas, Thaddaeus","summary":"Breast, the name of one of the apostles (Mark 3:18), called “Lebbaeus” in Matt. 10:3, and in Luke 6:16, “Judas the brother of James;” while John (14:22), probably referring to the same person, speaks of “Judas, not Iscariot.”","verses":5,"father":"Alphaeus","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"alphaeus_192","name":"Alphaeus","also":"","summary":"The father of James the Less, the apostle and writer of the epistle (Matt. 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13), and the husband of Mary (John 19:25).","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"bartholomew_405","name":"Bartholomew","also":"","summary":"Son of Tolmai, one of the twelve apostles (Matt. 10:3; Acts 1:13); generally supposed to have been the same as Nathanael. In the synoptic gospels Philip and Bartholomew are always mentioned together, while Nathanael is never mentioned; in the fourth gospel, on the other hand, Philip and Nathanael are similarly mentioned together, but nothing is said of Bartholomew.","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"simon_2746","name":"Simon","also":"","summary":"One of the twelve apostles, called the Canaanite (Matt. 10:4; Mark 3:18). This word “Canaanite” does not mean a native of Canaan, but is derived from the Syriac word Kanean or Kaneniah, which was the name of a Jewish sect.","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[18]},{"id":"judas_1760","name":"Judas","also":"","summary":"Son of Simon (John 6:71; 13:2, 26), surnamed Iscariot, i.e., a man of Kerioth (Josh. 15:25). His name is uniformly the last in the list of the apostles, as given in the synoptic (i.e., the first three) Gospels.","verses":23,"father":"","mother":"","v":[19]},{"id":"satan_2476","name":"Satan","also":"Beelzebub","summary":"Adversary; accuser. When used as a proper name, the Hebrew word so rendered has the article “the adversary” (Job 1:6-12; 2:1-7).","verses":55,"father":"","mother":"","v":[22,23,26]},{"id":"holy_spirit_7400","name":"Holy Spirit","also":"Comforter, Holy Ghost","summary":"The third Person of the adorable Trinity.","verses":246,"father":"","mother":"","v":[29]},{"id":"mary_1938","name":"Mary","also":"","summary":"The wife of Joseph, the mother of Jesus, called the “Virgin Mary,” though never so designated in Scripture (Matt. 2:11; Acts 1:14). Little is known of her personal history.","verses":21,"father":"Heli","mother":"","v":[31]}],"places":[{"id":"galilee_433","name":"Galilee","kind":"Region","lat":32.70675,"lon":35.30153,"summary":"Circuit. Solomon rewarded Hiram for certain services rendered him by the gift of an upland plain among the mountains of Naphtali. Hiram was dissatisfied with the gift, and called it “the land of Cabul”.","verses":63,"v":[7]},{"id":"judea_657","name":"Judea","kind":"Region","lat":31.77744,"lon":35.23494,"summary":"After the Captivity this name was applied to the whole of the country west of the Jordan (Hag. 1:1, 14; 2:2).","verses":44,"v":[7]},{"id":"idumea_592","name":"Idumea","kind":"","lat":30.73469,"lon":35.60625,"summary":"The Greek form of Edom (Isa. 34:5, 6; Ezek. 35:15; 36:5, but in R.V. “Edom”).","verses":1,"v":[8]},{"id":"jerusalem_636","name":"Jerusalem","kind":"City","lat":31.77667,"lon":35.23417,"summary":"Called also Salem, Ariel, Jebus, the “city of God,” the “holy city;” by the modern Arabs el-Khuds, meaning “the holy;” once “the city of Judah” (2 Chr. 25:28).","verses":754,"v":[8,22]},{"id":"jordan_653","name":"Jordan","kind":"Water (River)","lat":33.18645,"lon":35.61916,"summary":"But besides these two historical fountains there is a third, called the Hasbany, which rises in the bottom of a valley at the western base of Hermon, 12 miles north of Tell-el-Kady.","verses":177,"v":[8]},{"id":"sidon_1093","name":"Sidon","kind":"City","lat":33.56024,"lon":35.37121,"summary":"A fishery, a town on the Mediterranean coast, about 25 miles north of Tyre. It received its name from the “first-born” of Canaan, the grandson of Noah (Gen. 10:15, 19).","verses":30,"v":[8]},{"id":"tyre_1184","name":"Tyre","kind":"City","lat":33.26807,"lon":35.20936,"summary":"A rock, now es-Sur; an ancient Phoenician city, about 23 miles, in a direct line, north of Acre, and 20 south of Sidon. Sidon was the oldest Phoenician city, but Tyre had a longer and more illustrious history.","verses":52,"v":[8]}],"events":[{"id":"healing_a_withered_hand_293","title":"Healing a Withered Hand","year":28,"summary":"Passage: Matthew 12:9 – Luke 6:11. People: Jesus. Place: Capernaum.","v":[1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"id":"healing_multitudes_294","title":"Healing Multitudes","year":28,"summary":"Passage: Matthew 12:15 – Luke 6:19. People: Jesus. Place: Galilee.","v":[7,8,9,10,11,12]},{"id":"apostles_chosen_295","title":"Apostles Chosen","year":28,"summary":"Passage: Matthew 10:2 – Luke 6:16. People: Simon, Andrew, James, John, Philip, Ba